// An anchor file (§15.1), stating exactly what `anchors::earth` states.
//
// # Why this is a separate file from earth.hjson
//
// §15.1 requires it, and gives the reason: parameters change with better
// measurement, anchors with re-determination. Those are different events on
// different schedules. A revised rotation period does not invalidate a phase,
// and a re-determined phase does not change a rotation period. One file would
// tie them together and make a revision of either look like a revision of both
// — which is what the `revision` field below exists to distinguish.
//
// # What an anchor file cannot be used to do
//
// Invent a phase. Rule J makes phase empirical: determined and cited, never
// derived and never borrowed from another body. Two of the twelve derived
// calendars that ship have an anchor, and that ratio is not a gap in the
// program — `D5-titan-anchor.md` records a full literature search that ended in
// "no anchor for Titan", because its rotational elements are published and its
// mean-solar-time convention is not.
//
// The loader adds no checks of its own and must not. Every refusal below comes
// from `Anchor::new`, which the compiled-in anchors go through as well:
//
//   - the phase must name a physical event of the body itself (Rule J.1), so a
//     custom description mentioning a foreign epoch, clock or calendar is
//     UCAL-E0063;
//   - the window must contain the tick (Rule J.2), because a window that
//     excludes its own best estimate is a contradiction and not an uncertainty
//     — UCAL-E0062;
//   - the determination must state a method, a citation, and what dominates the
//     uncertainty. None of the three is optional.
//
// So a file cannot reach a state a Rust constant could not. It can reach that
// state without editing the crate, which is the entire feature.
//
// # The window is stated, never computed
//
// Nothing here derives the window from an uncertainty, or the uncertainty from
// the window. A loader that widened or narrowed a window by a rule of its own
// would be narrowing by assumption, which GE-3 forbids and which is far easier
// to do accidentally in a file than in a constant.

calendar_id: earth-d

// Mean solar midnight at Greenwich on 2000-01-01, in ticks.
tick: 8070205173569172848597429796163475680530466139316558837890625

// +/- 1 ms about that tick. Both bounds written out; see above.
window_lo: 8070205173569172848578881211763614680530466139316558837890625
window_hi: 8070205173569172848615978380563336680530466139316558837890625

phase: {
  kind: mean_solar_midnight
  meridian: greenwich
  meridian_citation: BIPM, the Metre Convention and the treaty establishing the prime meridian
}

determination: {
  method: mean solar midnight at the prime meridian on 2000-01-01, i.e. 00:00:00 UT1, converted through TT = UT1 + Delta-T with Delta-T(2000.0) = 63.8285 s
  citation: IERS Conventions (2010) and the IERS Earth Orientation Centre's published Delta-T series; the original document is available in the Internet Archive
  locator: https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://www.iers.org/
  uncertainty_note: dominated by the resolution of the published Delta-T series, which is quoted to 0.0001 s near 2000.0; the window is widened to 1 ms to cover the series' own stated scatter
}

// Anchors are versioned because they are observations, and a re-determination
// is a new revision rather than an edit.
revision: 1
